nal trade of endangered  species we use this data a great deal to  understand the flow of wildlife across  the world  the organization has more than 40 years  of data of essentially every wildlife  trade between treaty countries that has  occurred but they didn't have a bulk way  to export it we needed to use it in bulk  so we built a package that that uses  these and we've just put up a first  version of it so we have our you know  version one of the data but it'll update  as we have new submissions decide ease  it updates on the order of months when  countries submit their trade data so  it's not a minute-to-minute thing but  its version and that's important for us  and essentially how it works is you just  have a date a function to call the data  which is site e's data if it's not  currently available in your library  it'll download that highly compressed  binary off of the repository in this  case github and you can specify the  version you want to load so that'll load  up it moves relatively fast but once you  have done that a first-time 1.0 is  cached on your computer and so when you  call it again it'll load up immediately  and so we have quick access to in this  case two million rows of data that is  easy to work with across projects anyone  can just add this function in and you  don't have to have you know  fragmentation of the datasets across  different things are working with  sometimes you don't have control of the  upstream component of your data so what  we often do is have a data design  pattern where we have pull from an API  but sync to a local database that we  maintain so there's an SQLite database  in our data library and for different  packages that in this case work with  taxonomic data or infectious disease  surveillance data we sync that data down  into local database you can work on it  and you just run sort of a single let's  download the current database function  it'll pull it down sync your look sync  what you have locally and you can work  with it then with the taxonomic database  we actually built originally an API  package to do the calls but because of  the slowness of that access we moved to  this design and so we actually have two  packages they have the exact same code  API so if in this case we're making a  call of in this case we're doing a  taxonomic lookup at the NCBI database  you know you can run it with an API call  it'll take a couple of seconds to pull  down the data of making HTTP requests or  you can do it with the version of the  package that maintains a local database  and what we pull up is 10 times faster  and actually scales much better too many  requests because you can vectorize the  requests locally but not to the API  other sort of helpers we use in packages  like this is giving a package status so  when you load up your package we show  the status of the local databases which  tables you have available what you've  downloaded when you've downloaded what  you can update and so this is helpful  it's especially helpful for our case  where the table availability depends on  one security permissions across the  shared project so maybe you'll have some  data and not others it shows what's  available when you load it up which is  helpful for an analyst one of the other  patterns we've been using across these  data access packages is in making  metadata useful so a lot of times when  there's a big data set available in a  public repository there'll be a code  book or a metadata sheet along with  it that has something like you know  descriptions of the codes that are there  the units that are available for the  columns the descriptions necessary and  it's often in a document like this a PDF  or a doc and it's there for good reason  because they expect people to read it  with their eyes they want people to be  able to scan it and look something up  but then you don't have the ability to  work with it in a computational way for  instance to link codes and descriptions  for visualizations so for the case of  societies and some other projects that  we've worked on in this case we use a  package called tabular the wraps the  tabula PDF library which can extract  that data into a structured tidy data  frame and so we every time we download  this PDF data it becomes tidy data so  you can work with that data but then  there's the reverse problem this tidy  data you know this data frame you know  contains a lot of values and you want to  be able to scan it quickly to search it  to use to use it for a lookup purposes  and so we had this approach where while  we have the data itself available within  a package we also build it into the help  files as an HTML widget so if you search  for help about the metadata of a package  it will pop up an HTML help file which  has a searchable data table you know a  lot of people don't realize that our  help system is capable of very rich HTML  markup you can have images you can have  forms you can have all sorts of things  you would have on a web page you know it  takes some rube goldberg  because you've got you know HTML and  JavaScript inside our documentation  files inside her oxygen comments and  there's a lot of escaping and fixing you  have to do but we implemented inside  ease I'm hoping to wrap it into sort of  a helper package for this purpose too  and then finally we provide analysts  with breadcrumbs so one of the things we  implement across things is print  functions that flag conditions in the  data that might be important for  instance if there are field notes  associated with the fields and the  subsets you pull up we show those flags  so that people know look at the notes or  they might show  there are duplicate fields in this data  and so that helps people as they're  exploring data but since it's only  involved in the interactive print  methods when you actually implement a  data pipeline you don't have the  performance hit of doing those lookups  and checks every time you print but it  helps people understand the data as they  get so in some if we're designing data  packages we have choices we can store  the data we can query the data or we can  sync the data and that choice really  depends on your data scale and your time  weight of your turnover  I think sync syncing version data is  something that is very useful and is  useful at the scale of data that a lot  of people work at it's helped with these  efficient formats like your feathers and  your FS T's and moving an SQLite  database back and forth and then when  you do that you can build rich  documentation and you can build  guideposts for your analysts along the  way  and all of these things help us sort of  minimize the trade-offs we have with  sort of archival storage problems and I  think as we develop a few more of these  tools we'll be able to connect with the  scientific backends with all of the  efficiencies that we get with these API  and syncing approaches so that's it  thank you very much  thank you  you
 

